    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d585">

                <pb facs="0886" n="876" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg876"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.93/9–2846: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-09-28">September 28,
                        1946</date>.<lb/>[Received September 28—6 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1557. Wu Teh-chen, Secretary General [of] Kuomintang, told United Press in
                    commenting on Gromyko’s recent charges to Security Council, <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“American GI’s certainly don’t jeopardize world peace by remaining in
                            China, but on the contrary actually help bring about peace.…<anchor
                                n="66" xml:id="d585-f1"/> I am of the opinion world peace would be
                            endangered only when foreign troops enter the territory of another
                            country and remain without sanction of its government.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Official <hi rendition="#italic">Central Daily News</hi> editorial September 27
                    took same stand as above.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
